WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:08.510
You may think you're getting a great deal,
but you could actually be getting ripped off.

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:15.850
Let's reveal the all time top retail ripoffs
of modern times.

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:18.380
1.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:26.900
Can you remember how much you spent on popcorn
the last time you visited the cinema?

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:32.630
A small bucket of popcorn usually costs around
$6 in the US or £4 in the UK.

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:39.829
But you may be horrified to hear that small
bucket of popcorn you just spent a fiver on

00:00:39.829 --> 00:00:44.970
only contains around 8 cents worth of popcorn
kernels.

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:51.630
It's not even justifiable, because when you
take into consideration all the costs of overheads,

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:57.969
including sugar, salt, cooking the popcorn,
staff wages and renting the premises, the

00:00:57.969 --> 00:01:06.390
popcorn is still being sold at around a 1,275
per cent mark-up.

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:13.270
Even more shockingly, the box that the popcorn
comes in usually costs the cinema more than

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:16.090
the actual popcorn inside it.

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:17.090
2.

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:23.409
I'm sure, like all of us, you've been a victim
of the sales scandal.

00:01:23.409 --> 00:01:28.590
It's hard to resist when you are being sold
the promise of an amazing saving on that new

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:32.640
kitchen, or snapping up 2-4-1 deals in the
supermarket.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:38.090
According to the law, a sale item only needs
to have been offered at the older, higher

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:44.869
price for 28 consecutive days within the previous
six months.

00:01:44.869 --> 00:01:50.689
In retrospect of this incredibly flexible
law, many companies will drastically increase

00:01:50.689 --> 00:01:57.030
the price of an item in a single store in
a remote location, where little of it is sold

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:58.030
anyway.

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:05.610
Then, 28 days later, market the item as on
sale at a seemingly unbeatable price across

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:07.890
the entire nation.

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:13.250
Despite the fact that the new sale price is
usually incredibly similar to what the item

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:16.130
was being sold at before the sale anyway.

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:22.910
In fact a British shopping comparison site
found that the 3 major kitchen retailers in

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:31.140
the UK had all increased the price of their
kitchens by up to 130 per cent in the run

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:38.520
up to Christmas, just so that when they went
on sale they could advertise a massive saving.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:43.720
When the sale price was actually just a few
pence cheaper than what they were selling

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:47.590
at only 2 months earlier.

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:52.640
Supermarkets are the worst culprits of this
rip-off, all those price slashes and savings

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:56.730
you see in supermarkets are rarely what they
seem.

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:57.730
3.

00:02:57.730 --> 00:03:02.880
I'm sorry ladies but you're being ripped off
on beauty products.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:09.440
Research shows that despite being virtually
identical, except for the smell and packaging,

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:16.350
on average women pay up to 75 per cent more
than men for the same products.

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:24.120
A well known razor brand was found to be selling
pink versions of the exact same razor to women

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:32.490
for $7, when the men's version was selling
in the same store for $5.70.

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:38.750
Similarly women's shaving gel has been found
selling at a price 70% more than the men's

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:41.720
version for the exact same product.

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:42.720
4.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:48.850
But it doesn't end there, women are also being
ripped off on a whole other level by the array

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:51.910
of anti-ageing creams on offer.

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:57.590
Anti-ageing creams are known for their significantly
expensive price tags.

00:03:57.590 --> 00:04:03.190
Big pharmaceutical companies play on women's
insecurities and emotions to sell to them

00:04:03.190 --> 00:04:07.980
what amounts to no more than a drastically
overpriced moisturisers.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:13.180
And it works, the anti-ageing business is
worth millions.

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:19.579
Adverts heavily use scientific sounding terms
to sound authoritative and sell more.

00:04:19.579 --> 00:04:28.930
Such as "These emollients are specially formulated
with aqua and humectants" which, when translated

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:37.660
from scientific nonsense actually means "These
lotions and creams contain water and moisturisers".

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:45.210
But the worst part is that dermatological
studies have repeatedly shown that anti-ageing

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:49.660
creams straight up don't do what they claim.

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:56.520
And they definitely don't reverse the signs
of ageing, at least not permanently.

00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:03.980
When asked, most Dermatologists agreed that
around 95% of claims made by anti-ageing products

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:07.190
are complete nonsense.

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:12.230
Sure they will make you look younger in the
short term, because they contain moisturiser

00:05:12.230 --> 00:05:16.320
which plumps up the skin, reducing the appearance
of wrinkles.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:23.030
But any cheap moisturiser will do the exact
same thing.

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:28.590
However once the moisturising effect wears
off, your face will look exactly the same

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:31.440
as before applying the product.

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:39.240
But the worst part is, anti-ageing creams
can actually speed up the ageing process.

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:45.690
Our skin has a natural shedding cycle, old
cells will eventually fall away and reveal

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:48.110
the news healthy cells underneath.

00:05:48.110 --> 00:05:55.290
Some anti-ageing companies abuse this fact
by adding active ingredients such as Hydroxy

00:05:55.290 --> 00:06:01.290
acids to their products, which speeds up the
shedding process, meaning the healthy skin

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:07.630
underneath will come to the surface faster,
making your skin appear to be smoother.

00:06:07.630 --> 00:06:14.750
But the use of these acids leaves the skin
more vulnerable to the Sun's harmful UV rays,

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:17.630
resulting in increased skin damage.

00:06:17.630 --> 00:06:24.410
So the use of anti-ageing products in combinations
with exposure to the sun can actually speed

00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:26.670
up the ageing process.

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:27.670
5.

00:06:27.670 --> 00:06:34.520
When you buy over the counter pain killers
such as Aspirin, Ibuprofen or Paracetamol,

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:41.060
which is known in the US as Acetaminophen,
do you choose the cheap unbranded version,

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:45.860
or the more expensive and heavily marketed
branded packet?

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:51.770
I'm sorry to say but you're paying a lot more
for the exact same thing.

00:06:51.770 --> 00:06:59.970
In the UK a box of 16 500mg paracetamol painkillers
sells for just 19 pence.

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:09.169
But the same number of tablets sold in a fancier
branded packet costs £1.45, thats nearly

00:07:09.169 --> 00:07:13.150
8 times more expensive.

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:20.050
Despite the fact that both products contain
the exact same amount of the active ingredient.

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:24.980
There is absolutely no difference in the pain
killing power of both products.

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:31.340
But it's not just paracetamol and it's not
just in the UK that this happens, most branded

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:38.770
pills are considerably more expensive all
over the world than their identical, un-branded

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:39.889
counterparts.

00:07:39.889 --> 00:07:46.550
However research has shown that, solely thanks
to the placebo effect some people do find

00:07:46.550 --> 00:07:49.650
branded painkillers to be more effective.

00:07:49.650 --> 00:07:50.669
6.

00:07:50.669 --> 00:07:57.330
No, it's not your imagination, your favourite
chocolate bar is shrinking.

00:07:57.330 --> 00:08:04.550
Each year big chocolate manufacturer's shave
grams of the size and weight of their chocolate

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:05.550
bars.

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:12.620
The idea is that if they reduce the size very
slowly consumers won't notice and will instead,

00:08:12.620 --> 00:08:16.669
obliviously get used to the new size.

00:08:16.669 --> 00:08:22.020
When asked why their chocolate bars have shrunk
over the years, manufacturers say it's because

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:25.720
of increasing pressure from public health
campaigns.

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:34.039
But the problem is that the price you're paying
remains exactly the same for less chocolate.

00:08:34.039 --> 00:08:44.110
In 2008 Snickers bars weighed 62.5g, but they
now weigh in at 48g, however the price remains

00:08:44.110 --> 00:08:45.820
exactly the same.

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:52.770
Huge chocolate manufacturers such as Hershey's,
Cadbury's, Nestle and Mars are all culprits

00:08:52.770 --> 00:08:57.200
of shrinking chocolate bars to increase profit
margins.

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:03.360
But it's not just chocolate, the amount of
air put into crisps packets is also increasing,

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:07.330
whilst the amount of crisps slowly decreases.

00:09:07.330 --> 00:09:13.380
Cereal manufacturer's are also making their
boxes skinnier so they hold less cereal.

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:14.380
7.

00:09:14.380 --> 00:09:18.270
Smartphone apps are pretty cheap, right?

00:09:18.270 --> 00:09:26.000
Not the I Am Rich app, this $1,000 dollar
app caused controversy in 2008 when it was

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:35.190
sold on Apple's app store for the highest
possible selling price for an app, $999.99.

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:39.680
It must have done something pretty special
for that price right?

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:45.100
Well not quite, all the app did was display
a glowing red gem on your screen when you

00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:46.210
opened it.

00:09:46.210 --> 00:09:53.390
The app description even stated it was a "Work
of art with no hidden function at all.".

00:09:53.390 --> 00:09:58.870
It was only on sale for 24 hours before Apple
pulled it from the store.

00:09:58.870 --> 00:10:07.630
Nevertheless during those 24 hours eight people
bought this app for a remarkable $999 dollars.

00:10:07.630 --> 00:10:08.680
8.

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:15.920
Here's a remarkable fact for you, 70% of grocery
purchases are unplanned.

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:21.680
Do you ever find yourself going to the supermarket
to buy a few ingredients and come out with

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:25.580
bags full of strange products you don't even
need?

00:10:25.580 --> 00:10:31.240
That's because supermarkets are intelligently
designed to maximise the amount of money you

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:32.420
spend.

00:10:32.420 --> 00:10:37.620
The most profitable products with the highest
profit margins are placed at eye level on

00:10:37.620 --> 00:10:44.390
the shelves, whilst the cheaper products and
the genuine bargains are placed on very low

00:10:44.390 --> 00:10:47.170
or very high shelves.

00:10:47.170 --> 00:10:52.500
Supermarkets also place the most expensive
products such as tempting electrical goods

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:58.640
at the start of the store and the cheaper
food goods towards the back, so you have to

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:04.670
walk through an array of tantalising electrical
goods before you pickup your groceries.

00:11:04.670 --> 00:11:10.370
Do you ever find yourself endlessly walking
in circles around the supermarket looking

00:11:10.370 --> 00:11:15.490
for that one product that you've purchased
before, and you thought you knew where it

00:11:15.490 --> 00:11:17.149
was located?

00:11:17.149 --> 00:11:23.279
You're not going crazy, it's because supermarkets
frequently move products around so shoppers

00:11:23.279 --> 00:11:29.980
have to traipse around the whole store looking
for them, all the while passing hundreds of

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:37.060
other tempting products which they may just
throw in the trolley and unwittingly purchase.

00:11:37.060 --> 00:11:38.410
9.

00:11:38.410 --> 00:11:43.850
If you've ever bought wine at a restaurant
you will surely know how expensive it can

00:11:43.850 --> 00:11:44.850
be.

00:11:44.850 --> 00:11:49.631
And you may think you're getting a premium
bottle of wine for your money, but you're

00:11:49.631 --> 00:11:50.750
not.

00:11:50.750 --> 00:11:53.800
The markup on wine in restaurants is insane.

00:11:53.800 --> 00:12:01.910
At most restaurants the menu price for a bottle
of wine is usually around a 200% markup compared

00:12:01.910 --> 00:12:03.450
to its store price.

00:12:03.450 --> 00:12:13.920
And buying wine by the glass is even worse,
you will pay a 400% markup on a glass of wine.

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:20.680
To make it even worse, the cheapest wines
on the menu usually have the highest markups

00:12:20.680 --> 00:12:24.649
to make them seem like more expensive wines.

00:12:24.649 --> 00:12:30.830
Some restaurants allow you to bring your own
alcohol, if so you will save yourself a considerable

00:12:30.830 --> 00:12:32.660
amount on the bill.

00:12:32.660 --> 00:12:33.660
10.

00:12:33.660 --> 00:12:36.920
And finally, is Hotel mini bars.

00:12:36.920 --> 00:12:42.140
It's become somewhat of a cliche, don't touch
the mini bar, it will cost a fortune.

00:12:42.140 --> 00:12:44.930
But the fear is real.

00:12:44.930 --> 00:12:51.100
On average beverages and snacks taken from
hotel mini bars cost around four times as

00:12:51.100 --> 00:12:55.180
much as what you would pay at the supermarket.

00:12:55.180 --> 00:13:03.770
One New York City hotel even charged a 1,300
per cent markup for a bag of Gummi bears.

00:13:03.770 --> 00:13:10.350
And to rub salt in the wound, today the charges
are automatic.

00:13:10.350 --> 00:13:16.230
Modern mini-bars have detectors that record
the items you remove and automatically bill

00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:18.620
it to your room.

00:13:18.620 --> 00:13:24.460
Hotel room movies also have ridiculous markups,
you can rent a movie from online services

00:13:24.460 --> 00:13:32.350
at home for only a couple of dollars, but
hotels routinely charge around $15 to rent

00:13:32.350 --> 00:13:39.149
the exact same movie in your hotel room.

